    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Evaluating the Offer\n\nOn paper, this offer is roughly **flat to slightly negative** compared to your current compensation, and taking it as-is would represent a financial step backward:\n\n* **Base Salary ($215,000 vs. $240,000):** You are taking an immediate **$25,000 (10.4%) pay cut** in guaranteed cash. The posted base range tops out at $271,000, meaning Google has substantial room to move up.\n* **First-Year Total Compensation ($522,000 vs. $526,000):**\n  * Google uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**). \n  * Year 1 Equity: $800,000 \u00d7 33% = **$264,000**.\n  * Year 1 Cash: $215,000 (Base) + $43,000 (Target Bonus) = **$258,000**.\n  * Year 1 Total Comp: **~$522,000** (virtually identical to your Meta run rate of $526,000).\n* **The \"Year 3 & 4 Cliff\":** Because Google front-loads the initial grant, your initial equity drops to $176k in Year 3 and $96k in Year 4. While annual refreshes help fill this gap over time (as the recruiter noted), you are taking on uncommitted risk to match what you already make.\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($0):** Leaving a company you are happy at without a sign-on bonus to offset the transition and timing of your next Meta bonus/vesting schedule leaves money on the table.\n\n**Verdict:** **Do not accept this offer as-is.** Since Google recruited you while you are happy and thriving at Meta, you have significant leverage.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: What and How Much to Ask For\n\nTo justify the risk and friction of changing companies, target a **15%\u201320% bump in Year 1/Year 2 total compensation**, protect your base salary, and bridge any transition costs.\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Google Offer | Counter-Offer Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $215,000 | **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000** *(near upper third of range)* |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $43,000 (20%) | **$49,000 \u2013 $50,000** *(20% of new base)* |\n| **4-Year RSUs** | ~$200k\u2013$250k/yr | $800,000 | **$1,000,000** *(~$330k/yr in Y1 & Y2 under 33/33/22/12)* |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$522,000** | **~$699,000 \u2013 $730,000** (incl. sign-on) |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Negotiation Plan\n\n1. **Anchor on Your Current Satisfaction at Meta:** Reinforce that you were not looking to leave and are on a strong trajectory. Google must offer a clear financial premium to justify the move.\n2. **Never Take a Base Pay Cut:** Large tech firms routinely match or beat peer base salaries if the figure sits within their salary band (and $245k\u2013$250k is well within their $183k\u2013$271k range).\n3. **Use Unvested Equity / Bonus Cycle for Sign-on Leverage:** Explain that you have $200k in RSUs vesting over the next 12 months plus your upcoming bonus cycle, making a sign-on bonus necessary to make you whole.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Sample Negotiation Script / Email\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team throughout the interview process and am excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *As mentioned when you initially reached out, I wasn't actively looking and am in a great spot at Meta with strong performance and momentum. Looking closely at the numbers, the current offer would actually be a step down in guaranteed base pay ($215k vs. my current $240k) and essentially flat in year-one total compensation.*\n>\n> *For me to leave a high-impact role at Meta and walk away from my upcoming bonus and $200k in vesting equity over the next 12 months, the package needs to reflect a meaningful step forward. To sign, I would be looking for:*\n>\n> * * **Base Salary:** $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 (to match and marginally improve upon my current guaranteed cash within your posted band)*\n> * * **Equity:** $1,000,000 over 4 years*\n> * * **Sign-On Bonus:** $75,000 \u2013 $100,000 (to offset unvested equity and bonus timing)*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to this range, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and commit to joining the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Recommendation\n* **Negotiate first.** Google rarely pulls an offer simply because a candidate counters professionally with market-justified data.\n* **Walk away if they refuse to move significantly.** If Google refuses to at least match your base salary ($240k+) and bump the equity/sign-on to provide a real net gain, you are better off staying at Meta.",
